{:edb/db-before-t 10061 :edb/db-after-t 10062 :edb/tx-data [[13194139543374 50 #inst "2026-10-28T18:07:17.372Z" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186056544 1078 #edb/ref 17592186056546 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186057564 1078 #edb/ref 17592186057569 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186057570 1078 #edb/ref 17592186057573 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186059543 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2+(-0.4)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-2+22 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186059543 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2+(-0.4)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-2+22 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186059544 1074 "We know that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot t \\\\  & =-8-10+t \\\\  & =t-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186059544 1074 "We know that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot t \\\\  & =-8-10+t \\\\  & =t-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186060030 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨-3,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨7,9⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186060030 1071 "Let $u=⟨-3,2⟩$ and $v=⟨7,9⟩.$ Find $u\\cdot v.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186060030 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-21+18 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186060030 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-21+18 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186060031 1074 "Multiply corresponding components and add:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=(-1)(10)+(0)(2)+(6)(-5)=-10-30=-40.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186060184 1074 "Perpendicular vectors have dot product zero. Thus\n\n$$\nu\\cdot v=x^2+(-2)(2)=x^2-4=0.\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x=\\pm2$. Since $x>0$, the required value is $x=2$." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186060185 1074 "Since $a\\perp b$, their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=x^2-6x+9=(x-3)^2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $x=3$." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186060920 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we know that the dot product can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\cdot \\cos\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\nHowever, there is another formula for the dot product that we can use if the two vectors are given in component form. If the components of our two vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}a_x},{\\color{blue}a_y}, a_z \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}b_x},{\\color{blue}b_y}, b_z \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthen the dot product can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}a_x} \\cdot {\\color{red}b_x} + {\\color{blue}a_y} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}b_y} + a_z \\cdot b_z\n$$\n\nFor example, if we have two vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5},2 \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6},7 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nThen we can find the dot product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-30+14 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The procedure for computing the dot product works for vectors of any number of components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5}⟩\\cdot ⟨{\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6}⟩ & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\cdot {\\color{blue}6} \\\\  & =12-30 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5},2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨{\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6},7,9⟩ & ={\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}+2\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =12-30+14+9 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186060920 1064 "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we know that the dot product can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\cdot \\cos\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\nHowever, there is another formula for the dot product that we can use if the two vectors are given in component form. If the components of our two vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}a_x},{\\color{blue}a_y}, a_z \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}b_x},{\\color{blue}b_y}, b_z \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthen the dot product can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}a_x} \\cdot {\\color{red}b_x} + {\\color{blue}a_y} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}b_y} + a_z \\cdot b_z\n$$\n\nFor example, if we have two vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5},2 \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6},7 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nThen we can find the dot product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-30+14 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The procedure for computing the dot product works for vectors of any number of components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =12-30 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3,-5,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6,7,9⟩ & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =12-30+14+9 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186060921 1064 "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a} &=3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{b}=4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =({\\color{red}3}\\mathbf{i}+({\\color{blue}-5})\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({\\color{red}4}\\mathbf{i}+{\\color{blue}6}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}+({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{i} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{j} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot |\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}}^{=1}+({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{i}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}}^{=0}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{i}}^{=0}+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\overbrace{\\mathbf{j}\\cdot \\mathbf{j}}^{=1} \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\cdot 1+({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\cdot 0+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})\\cdot 0+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =⟨{\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5}⟩\\cdot ⟨{\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6}⟩ \\\\  & =({\\color{red}3}\\cdot {\\color{red}4})+({\\color{blue}-5}\\cdot {\\color{blue}6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186060921 1064 "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=3i-5j,\\,b=4i+6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3i+(-5)j)\\cdot (4i+6j) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)i\\cdot i+(3\\cdot 6)i\\cdot j+(-5\\cdot 4)j\\cdot i+(-5\\cdot 6)j\\cdot j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot i & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,i\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}j\\cdot j & =|\\,j\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3\\cdot 4)\\overbrace{i\\cdot i}^{=1}+(3\\cdot 6)\\overbrace{i\\cdot j}^{=0}+(-5\\cdot 4)\\overbrace{j\\cdot i}^{=0}+(-5\\cdot 6)\\overbrace{j\\cdot j}^{=1} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)\\cdot 1+(3\\cdot 6)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 4)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186093788 1078 #edb/ref 17592186093793 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094225 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094228 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094231 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094232 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094243 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094247 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094244 1080 "AB^(→)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186094244 1080 "\\overrightarrow{AB}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094245 1080 "7BA^(→)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186094245 1080 "7\\overrightarrow{BA}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094246 1080 "-AB^(→)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186094246 1080 "-\\overrightarrow{AB}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094247 1080 "\\frac{1}{7}AB^(→)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186094247 1080 "\\frac{1}{7}\\overrightarrow{AB}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094248 1080 "-7BA^(→)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186094248 1080 "-7\\overrightarrow{BA}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186094939 1078 #edb/ref 17592186094943 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186095035 1078 #edb/ref 17592186095040 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186096969 1074 "Divide the vector by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\widehat a=\\frac{a}{|a|}=\\frac15a.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac15|a|=1$, and the positive scalar multiple has the same direction as $a$." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186097042 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $3$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $4$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186097042 1071 "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $3$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $4$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186097042 1074 "The horizontal and vertical vectors are perpendicular, so\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5.\n$$\n\nDividing by this magnitude gives the required unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}=\\frac15(a+b).\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186097043 1074 "North-east and south-east directions are perpendicular. Hence\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{20^2+13^2}=\\sqrt{569}.\n$$\n\nThe unit vector in the direction of $a+b$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{a+b}{\\sqrt{569}}.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186097045 1071 "The vector $AB^(→)$ has magnitude $7$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $AB^(→)$?" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186097045 1071 "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $7$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186097045 1074 "A unit vector in the direction of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ is obtained by dividing by its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{|\\overrightarrow{AB}|}=\\frac17\\overrightarrow{AB}.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is $\\frac17(7)=1$." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186097161 1074 "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. Reading the displacements from the diagram gives\n\n$$\nq=\\left\\langle\\frac52,-\\frac32\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad r=\\left\\langle\\frac32,3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $q+r=\\langle4,\\frac32\\rangle$ and\n\n$$\n|q+r|=\\sqrt{4^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}2.\n$$\n\nThe required unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\frac{q+r}{|q+r|}=\\frac{2(q+r)}{\\sqrt{73}}.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186097177 1074 "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. The vector moves four grid squares right and seven down, so\n\n$$\nc=\\left\\langle2,-\\frac72\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad |c|=\\sqrt{2^2+\\left(-\\frac72\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{65}}2.\n$$\n\nDividing by the magnitude gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{|c|}=\\frac{2c}{\\sqrt{65}}.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186102114 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102119 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186102536 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102537 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186102602 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102607 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186102608 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102612 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186102626 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102629 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186105488 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =13\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186105488 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186105489 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-7)+(-1)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{53}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186105489 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-7)+(-1)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-7)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{53}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186105490 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{c}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-4)+1\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186105490 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin