lse] [17592186107073 1064 "Suppose that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $7,$ the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ has magnitude $6,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta=45^\\circ.$ Then the magnitude of the cross product $\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =21\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\sin\\theta$ is the formula for the area of the parallelogram with sides $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|$ and $|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|$ and the angle $\\theta$ between the sides. So the magnitude of the cross product represents the area of the parallelogram spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/27/2717ed1209f60dac12c3fcb07aea25f99c49b053fe0ce440b7ce996002a067c7.png)\n\n**Note:** Since in the formula for the area, we are interested only in the magnitude of the cross product, it does not matter whether we take $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\,|$ or $|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a}\\,|$ as these vectors have the same magnitude (but opposite directions)." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186107073 1064 "Suppose that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $7,$ the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ has magnitude $6,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta=45^\\circ.$ Then the magnitude of the cross product $\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}||\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =21\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\sin\\theta$ is the formula for the area of the parallelogram with sides $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|$ and $|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|$ and the angle $\\theta$ between the sides. So the magnitude of the cross product represents the area of the parallelogram spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ as shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/27/2717ed1209f60dac12c3fcb07aea25f99c49b053fe0ce440b7ce996002a067c7.png)\n\n**Note:** Since in the formula for the area, we are interested only in the magnitude of the cross product, it does not matter whether we take $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf{b}\\,|$ or $|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{a}\\,|$ as these vectors have the same magnitude (but opposite directions)." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186107174 1064 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =OP \\\\  & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186107174 1064 "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186107175 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\cdot \\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186107175 1064 "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186107177 1064 "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ,$ then $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186107177 1064 "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ,$ then $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186122240 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122241 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186123038 1078 #edb/ref 17592186123040 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186124247 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-(\\operatorname{cos}y+(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}y)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute $(x,y)= (1,\\pi)$ into the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f(1,\\pi ) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\pi \\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-(\\operatorname{cos}\\pi +(1-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}\\pi )\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186124247 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatornam