e{cos}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\ t(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186125734 1064 "Given a level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0),$ a **tangent vector** is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x_0,y_0) = f_y(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo understand why the above vector is tangent to the level curve, first recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(x_0,y_0)$ is normal to the level curve. Now, by computing the dot product, we see that the vector $\\mathbf t$ is orthogonal to the gradient vector $\\nabla\\! f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\hspace{-0.167em}f\\cdot \\mathbf{t} & =({f}_{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})\\cdot ({f}_{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & ={f}_{x}{f}_{y}-{f}_{y}{f}_{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf t$ is tangent to the level curve." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186125734 1064 "Given a level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0),$ a **tangent vector** is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x_0,y_0) = f_y(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo understand why the above vector is tangent to the level curve, first recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(x_0,y_0)$ is normal to the level curve. Now, by computing the dot product, we see that the vector $\\mathbf t$ is orthogonal to the gradient vector $\\nabla\\! f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f\\cdot t & =({f}_{x}\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}\\text{ }j)\\cdot ({f}_{y}\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}j) \\\\  & ={f}_{x}{f}_{y}-{f}_{y}{f}_{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf t$ is tangent to the level curve."