cc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186129503 1074 "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-2{v}^{2} \\\\ u-v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -4v \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-1)-(-4v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2u+4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2u+4v & <0 \\\\ 4v & <2u \\\\ v & <\\frac{1}{2}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{2}u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/09/09f7f36ecc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186129505 1074 "Choose $u=xy$ and $v=y/x$. The boundary curves become $1\\le u\\le2$ and $1\\le v\\le2$. In the first quadrant this transformation is one-to-one, with inverse $x=\\sqrt{u/v}$ and $y=\\sqrt{uv}$. Its Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}=\n\\begin{vmatrix}y&x\\\\-y/x^2&1/x\\end{vmatrix}\n=\\frac{2y}{x}=2v.\n$$\n\nUsing the absolute inverse Jacobian,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_1^2\\int_1^2\\frac1{2v}\\,dv\\,du=\\frac12\\ln2.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186129506 1074 "The given substitution turns the boundaries into $1\\le u\\le2$ and $1\\le v\\le4$. It is one-to-one in the first quadrant: $x=(v/u)^{1/3}$ and $y=u^{1/3}v^{2/3}