xt{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186149117 1078 #edb/ref 17592186149119 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186151277 1078 #edb/ref 17592186151281 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186152431 1074 "The diagram shows an ellipse centered at the origin, with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5$. A parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIndeed, these coordinates satisfy $x^2/9+y^2/25=1$ and trace the entire ellipse. Swapping the radii would produce a horizontally stretched ellipse." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186152432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149123 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186152795 1074 "The dashed lines locate the center at $(2,1)$. The horizontal endpoints are $x=-2$ and $x=6$, while the vertical endpoints are $y=-1$ and $y=3$. Hence the radii are $4$ horizontally and $2$ vertically. Therefore\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta+1,\\qquad0\\le\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186152796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151283 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186168513 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168514 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186168519 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168520 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186168615 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168620 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186171710 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}4\\sqrt{2}uv\\hspace{0.167em}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186171710 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| = 2\\sqrt{2}u.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(z-y)\\,{|}_{x={u}^{2}\\,y=-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =v-(-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4\\sqrt{2}uv\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186171711 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =x-y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\sqrt{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186171711 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =x-y\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=u-v\\,z=v} \\\\  & =(u+v)-(u-v) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we denote \n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\},\n$$\n\nour surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2v\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,2v\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2v\\,\\text{d}v\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}1\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot [{v}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\cdot [u]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186172275 1074 "At the three indicated points, evaluate the components of $F(x,y)=\\langle x^2,-2y\\rangle$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(-2,1)&=\\langle4,-2\\rangle,\\\\\nF(2,3)&=\\langle4,-6\\rangle,\\\\\nF(0,-2)&=\\langle0,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matching diagram has a right-and-down arrow from each of the first two points and an upward arrow from $(0,-2)$, with the displayed component displacements." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186172276 1074 "Every arrow points downward. Its horizontal component points left for $x<0$, vanishes on the $y$-axis, and points