'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186179963 1074 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{