{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180019 1074 "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F