1071 "![](images/77/772added7ce46ba0ec25de66dfd03e0107787d851006f261ac8843eba008a2d2.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180032 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is false, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $F$ acts perpendicular to $n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180032 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf{F}$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is positive” is false, while statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf{n}$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7a469a331a3cda238fcefb9e7163743483975c4ecc7b962d6d5f8d79736693c.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/29/29f144d6f3ce8233a034025fe933bc57dbea5476c50a259853a98bd2a2b3a47b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is zero” only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180033 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j?$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180033 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}?$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180034 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180034 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf{n}=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$*" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180034 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180034 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/38/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (x\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180035 1071 "![](images/b3/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180035 1071 "![](images/b3/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180035 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180035 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{i}.$\n\n![](images/c4/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180036 1071 "![](images/19/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180036 1071 "![](images/19/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180036 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180036 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s\\hspace{1em}⟹\\hspace{1em}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180037 1071 "![](images/c4/c4e47604540f3cfb3f73af34b0d3eb64bb649e9a42004d22eccc2dd9007de4fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180037 1071 "![](images/c4/c4e47604540f3cfb3f73af34b0d3eb64bb649e9a42004d22eccc2dd9007de4fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180042 1071 "![](images/5c/5c8aef7238da3bb5aed0fd2a458f6fd9bcd42884bbd350616e58e7a25b1f0fe2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as 