 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0+0=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180084 1074 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0+0=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180085 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j?$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180085 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}?$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180088 1071 "![](images/55/55957095fc97f1a5b56f7bed1c6715c1df5fc2c7aee707b2c2e4d7ed34d9dce8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180088 1071 "![](images/55/55957095fc97f1a5b56f7bed1c6715c1df5fc2c7aee707b2c2e4d7ed34d9dce8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180091 1071 "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(-1,-1)$ for the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180091 1071 "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(-1,-1)$ for the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180091 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(-1,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is the $-1$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b803554d852d985c8feca61804a9e52423391fc4624a01c4199d0744fbf2e28d.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180091 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(-1,-1) & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is the $-1$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the streamline that passes through $P(-1,-1)$ is $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b803554d852d985c8feca61804a9e52423391fc4624a01c4199d0744fbf2e28d.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180093 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=4xy\\,i+{y}^{4}\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,i+3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)={e}^{xy}\\,i-y{e}^{x}\\,j$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180093 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=4xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}(3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\operatorname{sin}(3x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180173 1071 "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180173 1071 "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180173 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180173 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x-y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-t-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t) & =(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{\\mathbf{2}}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{2}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180175 1071 "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180175 1071 "![](images/51/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab