 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t) &=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)) & =x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t) & =(t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\mathbf{F}({\\mathbf{r}}_{\\mathbf{1}}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}_{1}^{'}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180177 1074 "Parametrize the segment by $x=1+t$, $y=2-t$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Its speed is $\\sqrt2$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C2xy\\,ds\n&=2\\sqrt2\\int_0^1(1+t)(2-t)\\,dt\\\\\n&=2\\sqrt2\\left[2t+\\frac{t^2}2-\\frac{t^3}3\\right]_0^1\n=\\frac{13\\sqrt2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180278 1071 "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\sin \\left(\\frac{πy}{4}\\right)ds\n$$ along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 2, 3)$." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180278 1071 "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}\\sin \\left(\\frac{πy}{4}\\right)ds\n$$ along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 2, 3)$." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180278 1074 "Use $r(t)=\\langle1+t,2t,1+2t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$. Its speed is $3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}4\\right)\\,ds\n&=3\\int_0^1\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}2\\right)\\,dt\\\\\n&=-\\frac6\\pi\\left[\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}2\\right)\\right]_0^1\n=\\frac6\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180279 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180279 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180280 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC=\\{-{C}_{1}\\}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180280 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC=\\{-{C}_{1}\\}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{1}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180281 1074 "The segment lies on $y=0$. Parametrize it by $x=1-2t$, $y=0$, $0\\le t\\le1$, giving $ds=2\\,dt$. Then\n\n$$\n\\int_C(2x-3y)\\,ds=\\int_0^1(2-4t)\\,2\\,dt\n=\\left[4t-4t^2\\right]_0^1=0.\n$$\n\nThe positive and negative contributions cancel. The arc-length factor is positive even though $x$ decreases." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180284 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t)^{2}\\cdot 3-(1+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}27{t}^{2}-2-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[9{t}^{3}-2t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[9(1)^{3}-2(1)-2(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186180284 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=3\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t)^{2}\\cdot 3-(1+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}27{t}^{2}-2-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[9{t}^{3}-2t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[9(1)^{3}-2(1)-2(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186180286 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) &=x(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},\\hspace{2em}t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}+2(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d