erse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{1+(x-y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}[u]_{0}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{1+{v}^{2}}\\,dv \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186199465 1064 "Recall that the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a smooth, orientable surface $S$ with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} \\,\\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}$ is a unit normal vector that defines the orientation of the surface.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa63a1e4f1d278cef6c53600e8fb9c60829de972ba7409afdc32837ecbc3e07.png)\n\nIf $S$ is given by a parametric equation of the form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v), \\qquad (u,v)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,\n$$\n\nwe can determine the unit normal vector using the fundamental product as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v}{\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\|}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into the formula for the flux, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}}{‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}}{‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖}\\,‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $S$ is a smooth, orientable surface given in parametric form by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D  \\mathbf F(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186199465 1064 "Recall that the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a smooth, orientable surface $S$ with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} \\,\\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}$ is a unit normal vector that defines the orientation of the surface.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa63a1e4f1d278cef6c53600e8fb9c60829de972ba7409afdc32837ecbc3e07.png)\n\nIf $S$ is given by a parametric equation of the form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v), \\qquad (u,v)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,\n$$\n\nwe can determine the unit normal vector using the fundamental product as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v}{\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\|}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into the formula for the flux, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\frac{{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}}{‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot \\frac{{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}}{‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖}\\hspace{0.222em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $S$ is a smooth, orientable surface given in parametric form by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D  \\mathbf F(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186233276 1071 "![](images/95/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e27