16{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}8{x}^{7}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{7}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[{x}^{8}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186235462 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nPreviously, we determined that our surface can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nand the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖=2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(x,\\theta )$ into $d(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(r(x,\\theta )) & =\\frac{x({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x((2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}d(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}d(r(x,\\theta ))‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}}\\cdot 2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8{x}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[{x}^{8}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186235513 1071 "Find the dot product of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}$ and $\\mathbf{r}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{r}=\\hspace{0.167em}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186235513 1071 "Find the dot product of the vectors $q$ and $r.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$q\\cdot r=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186235513 1074 "The dot product of two vectors given in component form can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{r} & ={q}_{x}\\cdot {r}_{x}+{q}_{y}\\cdot {r}_{y}+{q}_{z}\\cdot {r}_{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 1, we have $\\mathbf{q}=⟨0,-3,2⟩$ and $\