the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262619 1071 "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|=5$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262619 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262619 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262620 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨6,-4,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262620 1071 "Let $p=⟨6,-4,1⟩$ and $q=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262620 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}+(-4)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262620 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}+(-4)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}+1\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262661 1071 "![](images/70/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262661 1071 "![](images/70/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262661 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262661 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${\\mathbf{n}}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${\\mathbf{n}}_{1}=\\mathbf{i},$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot {\\mathbf{n}}_{1}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot \\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262687 1071 "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262687 1071 "![](images/95/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262687 1074 "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” is true, while statement “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative” is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/78/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive” only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262687 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple,