n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262701 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262724 1071 "![](images/ea/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186262724 1071 "![](images/ea/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186262724 1074 "If $F$ is a vect