\mathrm{∇}g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}(xy))(y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j})+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+y\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}]+[x\\operatorname{ln}(xy)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}+x\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+x(\\operatorname{ln}(xy)+1)\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186335800 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }i]+[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }j] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186335802 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186335802 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186335802 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $0$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186335802 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x