bf{i}+\\frac{y}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}2xy+\\frac{1}{3}xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}\\frac{7}{3}xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186363031 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3} \\\\ xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(-1,-1)={f}_{y}(-1,-1)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(-1,-1)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =(x+3{y}^{2})i-(y+2x)j \\\\ t(-1,-1) & =(-1+3(-1)^{2})i-(-1+2(-1))j \\\\  & =2\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186363031 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3} \\\\ xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,-1)={f}_{y}(-1,-1)i-{f}_{x}(-1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =(x+3{y}^{2})i-(y+2x)j \\\\ t(-1,-1) & =(-1+3(-1)^{2})i-(-1+2(-1))j \\\\  & =2\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+3\\,j.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186363033 1080 "3\\,\\mathbf{j}" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186363033 1080 "3