\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=2+2x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2x-y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-y,\\hspace{0.222em}z,\\hspace{0.222em}-2x⟩{|}_{z=2+2x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-y}_{P},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{2+2x-y}_{Q},\\hspace{0.222em}\\underbrace{-2x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2)-(2+2x-y)\\cdot (-1)-2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2+2x-y-2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\te