ac{∂Q}{∂y}=2xy+(-2)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186372174 1074 "A vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y{e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{y}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y{e}^{x}+x{e}^{y}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ has a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-2y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2xy+(-2)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)$ $=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i$ $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ only." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186372181 1071 "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(0,\\pi )$ for the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186372181 1071 "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(0,\\pi )$ for the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186372181 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(0,\\pi ) & ={e}^{(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is the $0$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our possible streamlines are $y=k\\pi $ for any integer $k.$ The streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is $y=\\pi .$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bd732367a876d2e6bba6bf96728ebcf8430176d2f480bff30a054cac5389b1f2.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186372181 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$