ve\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186379931 1071 "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=10$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186379931 1071 "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|=10$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186379931 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186379931 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186379938 1071 "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=2$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186379938 1071 "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|=2$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186379938 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186379938 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186380062 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186380062 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186380062 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{({x}^{2})}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186380062 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+(x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)