tor field $\\mathbf{F}$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186389346 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186389346 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ over the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186389346 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186389346 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186395845 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186395845 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186395845 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 2(y-3) \\\\  & =4(y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4(y-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nNow, we solve $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4(y-3)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}=0\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+0\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} &=0 \\\\ 4(y-3) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}$ at the point $(0,3).$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186395845 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 2(y-3) \\\\  & =4(y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} &=0 \\\\ 4(y-3) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,3).$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 false] [17592186398228 1074 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2-3t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({\\color{blue}4-9t})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139543374 17592186576411 true] [17592186398228 1074 "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{