ce $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ into $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) & =⟨3x,\\hspace{0.222em}3y,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩{|}_{x=u\\operatorname{cos}v\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}v\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $\\mathbf{F}$ and ${\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}) & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}3u\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v,\\hspace{0.222em}u⟩ \\\\  & =3u\\operatorname{cos}v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}v)+3u\\operatorname{sin}v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}v)+{u}^{2}\\cdot u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}v+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}v)+{u}^{3} \\\\  & =-5{u}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S} & ={∬}_{D}\\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v))\\cdot ({\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}-5{u}^{3}}\\hspace{